+Preserve the original wording and identifiers of supplied requirements; link any interpretation or proposed implementation as a separate item rather than rewriting the source requirement. A proposed architecture decision or safeguard remains ENGINEERING RECOMMENDATION unless an explicit supplied user, organizational or contractual decision establishes it as a project constraint; record that source. Do not infer a project requirement from a stricter design choice, an assumption, or its usefulness in satisfying another requirement. Each backlog row must have one basis and a traceable origin: split mixed-basis requirements and proposed decisions into separate stable-ID items and link them. Before finalizing, check each PROJECT REQUIREMENT against its supplied source; keep unsupported proposals separate.
 
 Read [references/sources.md](references/sources.md) when making legal claims. Verify current official text using available host tools before treating an obligation as verified. Distinguish entry into force from application, and legislation from guidance, standards and proposals. A static index is not proof of currency. If verification is unavailable, state `LEGAL SOURCE NOT VERIFIED`, use provisional applicability and continue useful engineering work where possible. Do not infer blanket AI Act exemption from MIT/FOSS licensing.
 
